From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Thu, 9 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Read RT-11 object modules

What MACRO-11 writes and what LINK and LIBR read.  We already write the
format, from the linker side, in ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em; nothing
could read it back, so there was no way to check what we emit against
what DEC's own tools emit, and no way to link against a module or a
library that came from the machine -- SYSLIB.OBJ included.

This is the reading half's skeleton: format recognition, records and
their checksums, p-sects becoming sections, and section contents
assembled out of the TXT records.  Symbols and relocations are not
translated yet, so a module reads as sections with contents and
nothing else; that is already enough for objdump -h, -s and -d.

Which section a TXT record belongs to is not in the TXT record: the
RLD record before it carries a location counter definition naming a
p-sect and an offset, and that is what the TXT address is relative to.
So the relocation records have to be walked even though the
relocations themselves are not read, which in turn needs the entry
size of every RLD type.  Those sizes come from the manual, and were
checked against every RLD record in a real SYSLIB.OBJ: 166 records,
all consumed exactly, no bytes left over.

Note for when the writer is revisited: the p-sect relocation types in
that library are 13 and 14, which under our writer's numbering
(ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em, and the loader in libs/libppu) do not
mean a p-sect relocation at all.  Our modules therefore read back
correctly only through our own loader.  The table here follows the
real data.

Checked by reading all 112 modules of SYSLIB.OBJ: every section's
contents came back byte for byte identical to an independent
reconstruction, no TXT record fell outside its p-sect, and a .SAV, a
library and a C source file are all still correctly not recognised.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
